WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, Episode 128.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.300
It's July 11th, 2026.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.000
The universe has been busy.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.240
Let's get into it.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.120
Japan just completed the first test flight

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.340
of an experimental reusable rocket,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.320
landing it safely after launch.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.680
Japan's space agency launched and landed its RVX

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.120
experimental vehicle in the first test

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.560
of reusable technology.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.920
The flight lasted only seconds,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.600
but proved the basic landing sequence works.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.200
Engineers aim to reuse first stages repeatedly

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.500
to compete with lower-cost operators.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.700
The prototype uses a hybrid engine design

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.320
that allows controlled descent.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.080
Jackson conducted the test on Saturday

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.140
to achieve technology key to cutting launch costs

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.620
in a market dominated by space X.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.100
Next, the team plans higher altitude tests

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.900
to refine guidance software.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.880
Japan's successful landing of the RVX prototype

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.440
marks a concrete step toward routine first stage reuse.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.000
The short flight proved the guidance and landing legs

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.260
can survive the return through the atmosphere.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.980
Engineers will now examine how the hybrid engine performs

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.580
after multiple restarts.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.840
The next series of tests will push the vehicle

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.440
to higher altitudes and longer coast phases.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.700
How high will the follow-on flights need to climb

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.920
before the design is considered flight-ready

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.000
for orbital missions?

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.020
Now, on a completely different note,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.980
NASA released a planning document

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.920
for future commercial space stations.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.140
The document mixes cost-plus requirements

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.720
with firm fixed price clauses for commercial space stations.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.840
Industry observers note the hybrid structure

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.900
creates tension between flexibility and accountability.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.260
NASA intends the stations to succeed

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.060
the International Space Station after 2030.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.440
The agency has not yet selected operators

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.440
for the initial commercial modules.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.440
The planning document blends all the requirements,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.000
deliverables and clauses of a cost-plus contract

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.340
into a firm fixed price framework.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.400
Shifting focus, parabolas completed ground tests

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.380
of a propulsion system sized for small satellites.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.280
The Space Force is funding development

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.080
to give CubeSats independent orbit changes.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.160
The hybrid engine combines solid fuel

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.060
with liquid oxidizer for restart capability.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.600
Engineers report stable burns

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.420
across multiple throttle settings.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.300
The technology targets maneuverability for CubeSats

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.480
in support of Space Force objectives.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.140
Flight demonstrations are scheduled for later this year.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.540
On another front, standard digital cameras

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.840
are being qualified as science instruments

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.360
for the upcoming Artemis-2 lunar flight.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.440
Crew members will capture high-resolution images

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.540
of Earth and the Moon during the mission.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.160
The approach reduces development costs

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.140
while still meeting radiation and vacuum requirements.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.820
Data from these cameras will supplement

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.180
official mission imagery.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.020
Calibration tests are already underway

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.440
at Johnson Space Center.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.640
Next up, reflect orbital received permission

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.900
to launch a single demonstration satellite.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.100
The spacecraft will attempt to redirect sunlight

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.540
toward selected ground targets at night.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.960
Astronomers have raised concerns

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.740
about increased sky brightness during observations.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.280
The company must still secure launch

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.980
and orbital slot approvals.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.700
A second approval round will be needed

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.760
before any constellation expansion.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.980
The Federal Communications Commission

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.800
granted the approval despite widespread criticism

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.480
from the astronomy community.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.760
Turning to a different discovery,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.560
the central black hole in a nearby galaxy

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.920
is producing an unusual radio flare

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.500
never seen before in similar systems.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.080
Its growth rate matches expectations

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.340
for black holes in the early universe.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.620
Radio observations reveal jets interacting

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.180
with surrounding gas on short time scales.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.800
The galaxy lies close enough for detailed follow-up

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:02.920
with current telescopes.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.300
Researchers plan multi-wave length campaigns

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.620
to track changes in the accretion flow.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.620
This object offers a rare local window

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.080
into processes that shape the first supermassive black holes.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.700
In other news, historical catalogs from Ptolemy

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.620
and Alsoufi listed the star Theta Eridani

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.380
among the brightest in the sky.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.720
Modern measurements show it is now far fainter

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.200
than those rankings imply.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.960
Astronomers attribute the change

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.860
to intrinsic stellar evolution

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.800
rather than observational error.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.380
The star is a binary system

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.540
whose combined light once reached higher luminosity.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.920
Long-term monitoring continues

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.200
to refine the evolutionary timeline.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.620
The discrepancy spans more than 2,000 years

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.080
of recorded observations.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.880
If every star in the night sky were fixed in brightness,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.380
ancient catalogs would match modern lists exactly.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.920
Theta Eridani shows that assumption fails.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.560
2,000 years ago, the star ranked among

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.260
the 13 brightest visible to the naked eye.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.740
Today, it sits well down the list.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.980
The change happened because the two stars

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.500
in this binary system evolved at slightly different rates,

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.480
shifting their combined output over centuries.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.460
A single human lifetime is too short to notice the drop.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.580
Yet the historical record captures it clearly.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.480
We still do not know the exact masses

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.000
of the two components or the precise moment

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.880
their combined light began to fade.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.900
That missing detail keeps the system

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.560
on observing lists at professional telescopes.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.600
Moving along, the Marseille Observatory doorkeeper

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.740
recorded comet C slash 1,801 N1 in 1,801.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.320
Directors had trained ponds in observational techniques

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.840
after noticing his talent.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.920
He went on to discover 37 comets,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.220
still the record for visual discoveries.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.780
The initial find came during routine sweeps

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:02.820
of the evening sky.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.600
His logs remain valuable for orbit reconstructions

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.120
of long period comets.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.580
Ponds began his career in 1789 as a concierge

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.580
at the observatory.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.040
In a separate event, Saturn's 10th magnitude moon

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.180
crossed directly above the planet's north pole this morning.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.940
Observers with video equipment recorded the moon's shadow

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.800
moving across the northern rings.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.540
The event repeats roughly every 2.7 days

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:30.800
as Dioni orbits.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.420
High resolution imaging can capture fine details

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.740
in the shadows edge.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.180
The next similar alignment occurs in early August.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.440
Elsewhere, the Seattle Museum will present research

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.660
on nuclear electric systems for future Mars missions.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.760
Speakers will discuss power levels needed

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.760
for crude round trip trajectories.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.100
The event highlights ongoing work

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.980
at several NASA centers on reactor designs.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.440
Attendance is open to the public

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.940
with registration required.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.060
Materials from the presentation will be archived online afterward.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.020
The talk focuses on propulsion concepts

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.640
that could shorten travel times to the red planet.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:14.580
On the imaging side, over 700 individual exposures were stacked

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.020
to reveal the barred spiral structure

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.900
of the southern pinwheel galaxy.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.540
A convolutional neural network separated stars

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.760
from the galaxy for separate processing.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.320
Color calibration matched stars

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.900
to the Gaia catalog for accurate hues.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.600
The galaxy lies roughly 15 million light years away.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.660
The final image combines RGB, hydrogen alpha,

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:38.900
and foreground data.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.120
The southern pinwheel is Messier 83,

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.380
a barred spiral similar in structure to the Milky Way.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.160
In the morning sky, the moon, Mars, and Uranus

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.600
formed a tight grouping visible before dawn.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.600
Unedited images show the planets near the moon's limb.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.200
The alignment is a line of sight effect

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.400
rather than a true orbital grouping.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.480
Observers across mid-northern latitudes

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.880
could capture the scene with modest equipment.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.620
Similar configurations recur on monthly cycles.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.020
Shifting scenes, multiple tracked exposures

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.380
captured the signal constellation above lake limities.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.680
RGB and hydrogen alpha frames were combined

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.280
to highlight nebulosity.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:24.160
The foreground required separate processing

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.380
to balance star and landscape brightness.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.820
The site offers dark skies with dramatic alpine reflections.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.680
The photographer used a Tamron 17 to 35 millimeter lens

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.560
at F2.8 on a Canon 6D.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.080
The image blends three hours of RGB data

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.380
with additional hydrogen alpha frames.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:48.700
Finally, a European design targets heavy lift capability

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.280
comparable to current super heavy vehicles.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.100
Engineers emphasize rapid turnaround

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.120
and reduced production costs.

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.140
The vehicle would use methalox engines

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.720
and vertical landing legs.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.940
Development timelines remain tied

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.680
to European space agency funding decisions.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:09.620
Wind tunnel tests of the booster shape are already scheduled.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.520
The concept positions Europe to challenge existing dominance

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.640
in reusable heavy lift systems.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.560
Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of approvals

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.080
needed for any expansion of sun-reflecting satellite concepts.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:26.020
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

